WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.367 --> 00:00:02.702 line:0%
-Do you feel light-headed?
Charlie, look at me.

00:00:02.702 --> 00:00:04.037 line:0%
-She was trying to help him.

00:00:04.037 --> 00:00:05.705 line:0%
-Who?

00:00:05.705 --> 00:00:07.207 line:0%
-Ellie.

00:00:07.207 --> 00:00:09.541 line:0%
She was trying to help him.

00:00:09.541 --> 00:00:11.811 line:0%
She just wanted
to send him home.

00:00:14.014 --> 00:00:19.685 line:0%
Do you ever get
the feeling that...

00:00:19.685 --> 00:00:23.356 line:0%
people are incapable
of not caring?

00:00:23.356 --> 00:00:28.794 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:28.794 --> 00:00:31.097
People are amazing.

00:00:32.398 --> 00:00:33.566
-Welcome back to "Late Night."

00:00:33.566 --> 00:00:35.401
We're here with Brendan Fraser,
everybody.

00:00:35.401 --> 00:00:36.402
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:36.402 --> 00:00:37.736
That was a clip from "The Whale"

00:00:37.736 --> 00:00:39.072
with the aforementioned
Hong Chau

00:00:39.072 --> 00:00:41.407
doing exactly what you said
she's so good at.

00:00:41.407 --> 00:00:44.110
When did you...
As a young person,

00:00:44.110 --> 00:00:47.113
when did you decide that
acting was the path for you?

00:00:47.113 --> 00:00:48.114
-Pretty early on.

00:00:48.114 --> 00:00:50.516
My family traveled a great deal.

00:00:50.516 --> 00:00:52.518
My father's work was
with Tourism Canada,

00:00:52.518 --> 00:00:54.187
and so every three or four years
we were somewhere new.

00:00:54.187 --> 00:00:58.190
Our family lived in Europe
in the '70s.

00:00:58.190 --> 00:00:59.826
And so, that was Holland.

00:00:59.826 --> 00:01:03.495
And from there, we would take
holidays to the UK, London.

00:01:03.495 --> 00:01:05.264
It's where I first fell in love

00:01:05.264 --> 00:01:09.034
with museums and parks
and the theater.

00:01:09.034 --> 00:01:10.336
And we would --

00:01:10.336 --> 00:01:12.738
My mother would send us off
while they went shopping.

00:01:12.738 --> 00:01:14.240
And we'd get tickets

00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:15.741
to see anything we could
get our hands on,

00:01:15.741 --> 00:01:18.578
"Jesus Christ Superstar"
or, you know, "The Mousetrap"

00:01:18.578 --> 00:01:22.548
or whatever funny old mystery
that was onstage.

00:01:22.548 --> 00:01:26.552
And it opened up like
a giant toy box to me,

00:01:26.552 --> 00:01:28.388
and I thought,
"I want to do that."

00:01:28.388 --> 00:01:31.791
I want to be a part
of the stories that I read,

00:01:31.791 --> 00:01:34.194
and I show them to people,

00:01:34.194 --> 00:01:37.863
and it feels like
you're included in it

00:01:37.863 --> 00:01:39.532
and it's a living story.

00:01:39.532 --> 00:01:42.035
And I think that's around the
time that I first started --

00:01:42.035 --> 00:01:43.869
first sort of got
the bite for it then.

00:01:43.869 --> 00:01:46.372
-And did your parents
celebrate this choice?

00:01:46.372 --> 00:01:47.706
Were they excited
about the path?

00:01:47.706 --> 00:01:50.542
-Yeah, they were.
They were very supportive.

00:01:50.542 --> 00:01:55.548
My mom actually wrote the check
that got me into college

00:01:55.548 --> 00:02:00.553
when I got the last
absolute final audition

00:02:00.553 --> 00:02:02.555
before the Labor Day weekend

00:02:02.555 --> 00:02:04.891
to start the new semester,

00:02:04.891 --> 00:02:06.459
and I was stone-cold broke.

00:02:06.459 --> 00:02:10.463
And they didn't tell me
if or not it was accepted.

00:02:10.463 --> 00:02:13.132
And I called on the following
Monday morning

00:02:13.132 --> 00:02:14.466
to ask, you know,
"Am I coming in?"

00:02:14.466 --> 00:02:15.835
And they were like,
"What's your name?"

00:02:15.835 --> 00:02:18.304
And I told them, I heard someone
like going, "Doo doo doo,"

00:02:18.304 --> 00:02:19.805
you know, dropping phones
and that kind of thing.

00:02:19.805 --> 00:02:21.907
And they went and picked up
a card and went...

00:02:21.907 --> 00:02:23.242
Or, I heard them
with a Rolodex or something.

00:02:23.242 --> 00:02:24.410
Like, "Fraser? Brendan?"

00:02:24.410 --> 00:02:25.444
I'm like, "Yeah."
They were like, "You're in.

00:02:25.444 --> 00:02:27.647
Get over here, now, now!"
[ Laughter ]

00:02:27.647 --> 00:02:29.282
I was like, "Okay!"
So, it was off.

00:02:29.282 --> 00:02:30.949
-A real professional operation
they're running.

00:02:30.949 --> 00:02:32.452
-Totally. Yeah.

00:02:32.452 --> 00:02:33.886
-They must have put actors
in charge

00:02:33.886 --> 00:02:35.388
of running
the office as well.

00:02:35.388 --> 00:02:36.589
[ Laughter ]

00:02:36.589 --> 00:02:39.425
Now, you know,
this film received

00:02:39.425 --> 00:02:42.428
incredible response
at the Venice Film Festival.

00:02:42.428 --> 00:02:44.263
Standing ovation.

00:02:44.263 --> 00:02:46.265
I know, you know,
you've had a career

00:02:46.265 --> 00:02:49.402
where you've had many highs,
but did this feel special?

00:02:49.402 --> 00:02:51.403
Did this...
Were you standing there

00:02:51.403 --> 00:02:53.205
thinking, "Wow, this is
an incredible moment"?

00:02:53.205 --> 00:02:55.741
-I was thinking I might be out
the door real soon if I hurry.

00:02:55.741 --> 00:02:57.744
You know?
[ Laughter ]

00:02:57.744 --> 00:02:59.745
And, 'cause, you know,
clapping is hard.

00:02:59.745 --> 00:03:01.247
It takes a lot, you know.

00:03:01.247 --> 00:03:02.481
-When you -- Yeah.

00:03:02.481 --> 00:03:04.817
[ Laughter and applause ]

00:03:04.817 --> 00:03:07.987
-You know. Ow!
[ Whining ]

00:03:07.987 --> 00:03:09.656
-But you read about, you know,

00:03:09.656 --> 00:03:11.024
especially at these
film festivals,

00:03:11.024 --> 00:03:12.091
you sort of read about these

00:03:12.091 --> 00:03:14.093
seven, eight,
nine-minute ovations.

00:03:14.093 --> 00:03:16.128
-I think it depends on
who's holding the stopwatch,

00:03:16.128 --> 00:03:17.697
to tell you the truth.
-Okay. Got you.

00:03:17.697 --> 00:03:19.532
So there's maybe a little bit
of a heavy finger?

00:03:19.532 --> 00:03:22.869
-I think. They're Italian.
"It's a-seven a-minutes!"

00:03:22.869 --> 00:03:25.204
"It's a 25 minute
standing ovation!"

00:03:25.204 --> 00:03:27.673
-But I will -- you know, as much
as I wouldn't want to stand

00:03:27.673 --> 00:03:30.442
and clap for seven minutes,
I feel like we sometimes

00:03:30.442 --> 00:03:33.278
underestimate how hard it is
to stand for seven minutes.

00:03:33.278 --> 00:03:34.780
Because at some point,

00:03:34.780 --> 00:03:37.115
you can probably only go like,
"Thank you, thank you" so much.

00:03:37.115 --> 00:03:39.251
-I just broke out crying.
That solved all the problems.

00:03:39.251 --> 00:03:40.286
-Oh, that's perfect.

00:03:40.286 --> 00:03:42.288
Perfect solution.
There you go, everybody.

00:03:42.288 --> 00:03:43.856
Did you have a moment in your
career

00:03:43.856 --> 00:03:45.358
where you thought,
"Oh, I made it"?

00:03:45.358 --> 00:03:46.892
-Um...

00:03:46.892 --> 00:03:47.927
Nope!
-Okay.

00:03:47.927 --> 00:03:49.028
[ Laughter ]

00:03:49.028 --> 00:03:50.362
-I don't.

00:03:50.362 --> 00:03:52.331
I feel like I'm still waiting
for someone to walk in

00:03:52.331 --> 00:03:55.668
and tell me that
the jig is up, you know.

00:03:55.668 --> 00:03:57.536
They haven't found me out yet,
but I know

00:03:57.536 --> 00:04:00.439
that I'm doing what I love,
and it's a privilege.

00:04:00.439 --> 00:04:03.109
I'm really happy
to be able to do that.

00:04:03.109 --> 00:04:06.445
-You, um...
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:04:06.445 --> 00:04:10.382
I know that obviously we talked
about the difficulties

00:04:10.382 --> 00:04:14.053
and how arduous it is to go
through the amount of hours

00:04:14.053 --> 00:04:15.588
in the makeup chair
for these prosthetics.

00:04:15.588 --> 00:04:17.089
-Yeah.
-With that said, I know,

00:04:17.089 --> 00:04:20.592
you know, you also have been
in films with a lot of stunts --

00:04:20.592 --> 00:04:21.894
"Journey," obviously,

00:04:21.894 --> 00:04:24.330
and "The Mummy,"
"George of the Jungle."

00:04:24.330 --> 00:04:28.101
Did you -- Was it nice to be in
a film where you didn't have to

00:04:28.101 --> 00:04:30.769
worry about hurting yourself
doing a stunt?

00:04:30.769 --> 00:04:32.271
-Oh, just my feelings,
that's all.

00:04:32.271 --> 00:04:34.774
[ Laughter ]
-Well, that's...

00:04:34.774 --> 00:04:36.075
Oh, feelings,
we got plenty of those.

00:04:36.075 --> 00:04:37.743
-I got more.
-[ Laughs ]

00:04:37.743 --> 00:04:39.745
Hey, it is so nice
to see you again.

00:04:39.745 --> 00:04:41.914
Thank you so much for being here
and congrats on the film.

00:04:41.914 --> 00:04:44.083
-Thanks, Seth.
-Brendan Fraser, everybody.

00:04:44.083 --> 00:04:45.418
"The Whale" is in theaters now.

